"""Original practical guidance extending the Phase 2 drafts."""
def section(title, *paragraphs):
    return (title, list(paragraphs))
EXTRA = {
'rpe-calculator': [
section('Separate the rating from the load decision',
'A rating describes the set you just completed. It is not an instruction to increase the next set automatically. Before changing weight, check whether the plan calls for repeated sets at a fixed load, a top set followed by lighter work, or an effort target for every set. Those are different prescriptions. An RPE 7 first set could be exactly right when the next three sets are expected to become harder as fatigue accumulates. Adding weight immediately might make the final set substantially harder than intended.',
'For a top-set approach, record the actual load, repetitions and rating together. Use the estimate as one piece of evidence for the back-off work. If the top set contains a missed pause or shortened range, do not let its apparently strong estimate determine the whole session. The calculator cannot recognise those changes. A coach reviewing the video may reasonably hold the load steady even when the arithmetic suggests an increase.',
'When repeated sets are prescribed at the same RPE, a small load reduction later in the session is not a failure. It can be the expected consequence of doing work. Record the reduction so that you can compare equivalent sets next time. Comparing the first set this week with the final set last week confuses fatigue with progress.'),
section('Use a consistent question after every set',
'Ask how many additional repetitions you could have completed with the same range, control and technique. That wording is more useful than asking whether a set felt hard. A long set can feel unpleasant because of breathing or local burning while several technically sound repetitions remain. Conversely, a heavy single can feel brief but leave no realistic second repetition. The rating should describe remaining repetitions, not how much you disliked the exercise.',
'Give the rating before opening the calculator result. Otherwise the predicted percentage can anchor your judgement: you may decide the set must have been RPE 8 because that is what the planned load implied. Keep the observation independent, then compare observation with prediction. A repeated mismatch is a reason to review the estimate, technique or exercise setup rather than force the rating to fit the chart.',
'Use the same convention for assisted repetitions. A spotter touching the bar changes what you completed independently. Do not record an assisted final repetition as an unassisted rep when estimating a max. Note the assistance and use the last clearly independent set instead. The same applies to a change in equipment, such as moving from a paused barbell bench to a machine chest press.'),
section('A practical review at the end of the week',
'Look for a pattern across comparable sessions. If the same load and repetitions repeatedly feel easier with consistent technique, that supports a modest progression. If one unusually strong session is followed by two difficult ones, a large jump based on the best estimate is harder to justify. The purpose of recording effort is to make the next decision better, not to produce the largest possible estimated maximum.',
'Write down what changed before interpreting the trend. A longer rest interval, different exercise order, straps, a belt, a new machine or a shorter range can all change performance. You can still use the session, but label it accurately. A simple note such as first exercise instead of third prevents a misleading comparison months later when the context is easy to forget.',
'For a client, keep the instruction short: finish the planned repetitions, stop when technique changes, and report how many good repetitions remained. The coach can handle the conversion into percentages. Giving the client several competing numerical targets at once often makes the session harder to follow without improving the information returned.'),
section('When to leave the estimate out of the decision',
'Do not use an effort estimate to clear pain, return from injury or justify a maximal attempt after an interruption. It estimates a relationship between load and repetitions under assumed conditions; it does not assess readiness or health. If symptoms, illness or an unfamiliar movement make the set unrepresentative, preserve the note and choose a more appropriate session with your coach.',
'A missing estimate is better than a precise-looking number built from poor inputs. You can still record the completed work and return to the calculation after a few consistent sessions. Keeping the conditions stable gives future ratings a useful reference and makes any eventual progression easier to explain.')],
'rir-calculator': [
section('Define what a remaining repetition means',
'A repetition in reserve must use the same technique as the repetitions already completed. If another rep would require bouncing, changing the range or asking a spotter to help, it does not belong in the reserve count for a strict set. Agree on that definition before comparing sessions. Two clients can use the same number while imagining very different standards, and no conversion table can correct that disagreement afterwards.',
'Reserve is specific to the exercise and the set. Being able to estimate two repetitions left on a familiar leg press does not guarantee the same accuracy on a new squat variation. Treat a newly introduced movement as a learning task. Start with conservative loads, review the movement, and build a reference from repeatable sessions instead of assuming the rating is already calibrated.',
'Technical failure is also exercise dependent. A free-weight lift may become unsafe or lose its intended pattern before the target muscle is unable to move anything at all. Stop at the agreed technical boundary. The calculator does not require you to reach failure to prove the estimate, and you should not deliberately compromise technique to test whether one more repetition was possible.'),
section('Interpret a range rather than a false exact point',
'A target of one to three repetitions in reserve gives useful room for normal uncertainty. A client who reports two may really have had one or three. That does not make the set worthless. It means the coach should interpret the report alongside repetition speed, technique, recent training and the rest of the session. Treat small differences as observations to investigate rather than immediate proof that the plan is wrong.',
'Half-point RPE values express an in-between judgement. RPE 8.5 maps to roughly one to two repetitions remaining, not to a literal half repetition that must be demonstrated. Use that precision only if it helps you describe the set. Someone learning the scale may provide better information with whole-number reserve targets and a short note about where the set became difficult.',
'High reserve estimates are particularly uncertain because the lifter stops far from the point they are estimating. The chart can still provide a starting weight, but it should not be treated as a measurement of a true maximum. A controlled set comfortably short of failure can be good training even when its calculated one-rep maximum has a wide margin of uncertainty.'),
section('Progress without turning every session into a test',
'Choose a repetition range and keep the reserve target consistent. If the plan says eight to ten repetitions with two left, first aim to perform the upper end cleanly at the intended effort. A small increase in load can then bring repetitions back toward the lower end. This is a coaching convention, not a promise that every session will improve. Record the actual result and let repeated performance guide the next change.',
'Do not add repetitions merely to make the calculator show a larger maximum. The useful observation is what happened under the plan you intended to follow. If you change the target halfway through every session, the log becomes a collection of different tests. Consistency makes comparisons easier and helps distinguish a productive increase from a change in execution.',
'Keep rest intervals reasonably comparable too. A short rest may lower repetition performance without indicating that underlying strength has fallen. If time is limited, record that constraint and choose fewer good sets rather than reinterpret every difficult set as a loss of fitness. The reserve target helps control effort, but it cannot erase the effect of fatigue accumulated earlier.'),
section('Discuss ratings without making the client defend a number',
'When a report looks surprising, ask what the final repetition felt like and whether the client thought another repetition would look the same. That conversation usually provides more useful detail than telling them their rating was wrong. A person who is worried about disappointing a coach may report the target rather than their experience. Make accurate reporting the goal, including when a set feels much harder than expected.',
'Video can support the discussion, but bar speed alone is not a universal reserve meter. Some people grind slowly before failure; others lose the movement abruptly. Use videos to compare the same person performing the same exercise over time. Avoid assigning everyone an exact reserve value from a single visual cue.',
'For remote coaching, put the cue beside the exercise in the plan and review the returned log. Keep the language identical across weeks. Changing between stop before it gets hard, RPE 8 and two reps left without explaining the relationship can create unnecessary confusion even though the intention is similar.'),
section('Account for exercise order and indirect fatigue',
'An accessory lift performed after several demanding compound movements may reach the same reserve target at a lower load. That is expected context, not necessarily a reason to replace the exercise. Compare it with earlier sessions in the same position. If you move it to the beginning, establish a new baseline instead of claiming an immediate strength increase.',
'A weekly review should include whether the target was achievable across the whole session. If the first exercise is consistently on target but later work becomes much harder, consider the total work, rest and exercise order. Adding more sets to compensate for lower loads can make the original fatigue problem worse. Change one part of the plan at a time so the effect is understandable.'),
section('Keep the result in its proper role',
'RIR describes effort in a resistance-training set. It is not a medical clearance, a measure of motivation or a diagnosis of poor recovery. Stop and seek appropriate advice when pain or other concerning symptoms appear. A client should never feel required to finish a reserve target through symptoms because a calculator predicted the load was manageable.',
'The strongest use of this tool is a shared vocabulary. The client reports a set in ordinary terms, the coach converts that information into the next prescription, and both can see the reasoning. The percentage is a starting estimate; the completed repetitions, technique and experience remain the evidence that matters in the next session.')],
 'tempo-calculator': [
section('Start counting from the correct phase',
'The four numbers describe lowering, the pause in the stretched position, lifting and the pause in the shortened position. They do not necessarily describe the chronological order of the first repetition. A squat commonly starts by lowering, while a deadlift starts by lifting from the floor. Read the phase labels before beginning instead of applying the first number to whichever movement happens first.',
'For an exercise that begins on the floor, explain how the start and reset fit the prescription. A controlled reset between repetitions is different from a long rest with the load fully supported. If the plan depends on continuous tension, make that intention explicit. The calculator adds the written phase durations; it cannot determine whether a particular pause keeps the target muscle working.',
'Count with a steady rhythm rather than trying to watch a clock during a demanding set. The aim is repeatable control. If the count varies slightly, record the completed repetitions honestly rather than pretending that every repetition lasted exactly the calculated number of seconds. A timing estimate is useful without being a laboratory measurement.'),
section('Separate repetition time from session time',
'The result counts the time spent performing repetitions. It does not include rest between sets, changing weights, setting up equipment, waiting for a station or warming up. For example, a set lasting forty-eight seconds followed by a two-minute rest occupies more than forty-eight seconds of the appointment. Build those other tasks into your session plan before deciding how much work fits.',
'Supersets can reduce some waiting time, but they do not eliminate fatigue or setup. Two exercises that need the same unavailable station may be impractical even when their repetition times look short. Use the calculator to understand the movement portion of a session, then review the actual gym conditions. A plan that fits on paper but requires constant rushing is difficult to follow well.',
'When comparing two tempos, hold repetitions and sets steady first. That shows the effect of timing alone. Then decide whether the extra duration is worth a change in load or total work. Altering tempo, repetitions and sets together makes it harder to identify what changed the session and whether that change served the intended purpose.'),
section('Teach the cue before prescribing several numbers',
'A beginner may understand lower for three counts, pause briefly, then stand up more easily than a four-digit code. Put both versions in the plan until the notation becomes familiar. The important part is that the client performs the intended movement. Correctly repeating a code without knowing which phase it refers to does not improve the exercise.',
'Demonstrate the pause position. On a squat, hovering just above the intended depth is different from holding the bottom position. On a row, pausing with the shoulder drifting forward is different from finishing the pull under control. The timer cannot decide whether the position meets your technical goal, so use a clear cue or demonstration when that distinction matters.',
'Give the client a way to report difficulty. If a prescribed pause makes balance or position deteriorate, they should be able to stop the set and ask for a change. Do not use a longer duration to force a position that is uncomfortable. A coach can shorten the pause, reduce the load or choose another exercise after reviewing what happened.'),
section('Read explosive notation as an intention',
'An X in the lifting phase usually asks for fast intent rather than an exact one-second duration. The actual movement speed depends on load, fatigue and the exercise. A heavy but deliberately fast attempt may still move slowly. This calculator represents the phase numerically for time estimates; that representation is not a promise about the actual time of every explosive repetition.',
'If measuring actual movement time matters for a specific training task, record it separately with an appropriate method. Do not infer bar velocity from the four tempo numbers. Two sets can use the same written tempo and have different movement speeds, pauses and technique. The notation is an instruction, not a sensor.',
'Keep explosive intent within the exercise and skill level you can perform safely. Fast lifting is not the same as losing control of the load or skipping the intended range. When a client is still learning the movement, a clear controlled repetition may be a more useful instruction than a speed target they cannot yet interpret.'),
section('Review tempo alongside the other training variables',
'Load, repetitions, range, rest and effort all affect the session. A longer set does not automatically provide a better stimulus than a shorter one. If slowing the movement means using substantially less weight or completing fewer technically sound repetitions, consider whether that trade serves the goal. Avoid selecting a tempo solely because it produces a larger time-under-tension total.',
'For progress tracking, keep the execution standard recognisable. If last month used a pause and this month does not, label the change. A larger load may partly reflect the easier execution rather than a pure increase in strength. Both sessions can be useful, but combining them into one trend without context makes the log less informative.',
'Use the result as a planning aid. It can help explain why a set feels long, compare two prescriptions and estimate how much movement time a session contains. It cannot tell you how much muscle you will gain, whether a tendon has recovered or which tempo is medically appropriate for an injury. Those questions require information beyond arithmetic.')],
'warm-up-set-calculator': [
section('Check the starting load against your equipment',
'The first row assumes a standard bar in the selected unit system. If you use a different bar, a machine with a starting resistance, or a dumbbell movement, account for that difference before following the ramp. A percentage that is mathematically sensible can still be impossible to load. Confirm the actual bar weight rather than assuming every bar in the gym is identical.',
'When the working load is close to the empty bar, several calculated warm-up steps may round to the same weight. You do not need to perform every duplicate row merely because it appears in a table. The table describes a general progression, and a coach can simplify it for a light working set. Do not add weight just to create a more impressive-looking ladder.',
'For machines, check whether the stack labels describe total resistance, plates added, or a manufacturer-specific setting. Cable ratios can change the relationship between the selected number and force at the handle. Keep the machine and setup consistent for tracking. Do not treat a machine number as interchangeable with the same number on a barbell.'),
section('Keep warm-up repetitions deliberate and comfortable',
'The early sets provide a chance to check position, balance and range before the heavier work. Use the same basic execution standard you intend for the working sets. Rushing an empty bar with a different movement pattern gives less useful information about readiness for the loaded version. A short technical cue can make the early repetitions purposeful without turning them into a separate workout.',
'The later warm-up sets use fewer repetitions because the goal is preparation rather than exhausting the target muscles. If a final warm-up feels unexpectedly difficult, pause before committing to the working load. Review the setup, rest and recent training. A calculator-generated target is not a requirement to complete a difficult set regardless of how the preceding work went.',
'Tell your coach about pain, dizziness or an unusual change in how you feel. Do not treat another warm-up set as a way to prove that symptoms can be ignored. The tool cannot distinguish ordinary effort from a reason to stop. Adjust or postpone the session when appropriate and seek professional advice for concerning symptoms.'),
section('Use the full and quick ramps in context',
'The full version gives more steps between the empty bar and the working load. The quick version uses fewer. Neither option guarantees readiness. A person returning to an unfamiliar lift may need extra rehearsal, while someone already warm from related exercises may need less. Use the selection to organise the transition rather than as a rule that overrides experience and coaching judgement.',
'Consider exercise order. A bench press after another pressing movement is different from the first lift after sitting all day. The specific warm-up still needs to introduce the exact movement, but general preparation may already be partly complete. Record the context when comparing how a load feels across sessions.',
'In a busy gym, plan plate changes before starting. Put the required plates nearby when permitted and keep both sides balanced while loading. A warm-up can become unnecessarily long if every step requires searching for a small plate. Rounding to available increments helps make the plan usable, but it should never replace checking that the bar is evenly loaded.'),
section('Preserve the working set as the main task',
'A warm-up is successful when it helps you begin the planned work with useful information and without unnecessary fatigue. More warm-up volume is not automatically better. If the early sets routinely leave you tired, review repetitions and spacing with your coach instead of adding another step because the working weight feels heavy.',
'After the first working set, record the actual performance. That gives the next session a stronger reference than the warm-up formula alone. If the planned load repeatedly needs adjustment, update the plan rather than repeat the same mismatch indefinitely. The ramp should follow a realistic working target, not conceal that the target is out of date.')],
'plate-loading-calculator': [
section('Read the result as one sleeve, not the whole bar',
'The plate list is for each side. If it says twenty-five plus fifteen plus one-and-a-quarter, put that combination on both sleeves. The total adds both sides to the bar. Counting the displayed list only once is a common mistake when moving from a written plan to the rack, especially when the target includes small plates.',
'Use a simple check before lifting: add one sleeve, double that number, then add the bar. Do the check independently of the target shown in your plan. This catches a missing small plate or a mistaken bar choice. A calculator can generate a correct combination while the actual equipment on the rack is different.',
'Collars secure the plates, but their weight may also matter. Some competition collars contribute a known amount, while ordinary gym clips are often ignored in everyday logging. This tool has no collar-weight input. If your target must include weighted collars, account for them separately and keep that convention consistent. Do not silently add them to the total on one session and omit them on the next.'),
section('What the available-plates setting assumes',
'Selecting a denomination means the tool may use repeated plates of that size. It does not record how many pairs you own. If your home gym has one pair of twenty-kilogram plates, a result using three per side is not loadable from that inventory. Check quantities as well as denominations before using the suggestion.',
'The default selection represents a conventional set of plate sizes, not a guarantee about your gym. Remove sizes that are unavailable. If the resulting load falls short, decide whether the nearest lower weight is appropriate for the session. Do not compensate by putting a different plate combination on the other sleeve. Both sides must remain balanced.',
'The routine selects larger denominations first. With the standard sets supplied here, that gives a familiar practical combination. It does not optimise for sleeve length, plate thickness, a limited number of pairs or the effort of changing plates between exercises. Those are separate equipment constraints. If one matters, review the combination manually before loading.'),
section('Plan changes between sets before you start',
'For a series of increasing loads, keep the heavy inner plates in place where possible and change the outer plates. The calculator shows each target independently, so its suggested arrangement may not minimise changes across a whole session. You can use an equivalent balanced combination if it makes transitions easier, provided the total is correct and the plates fit securely.',
'Consider the final working weight when arranging earlier warm-up sets. If a heavy plate will be needed later, it may be useful to have it nearby. That does not mean using a heavier warm-up than planned just to avoid a change. Preserve the intended progression and use the tool to check the arithmetic of each step.',
'When training with someone using a different load, agree on who checks each sleeve after a change. A small plate can be left on one side when several people are switching between sets. Repeat the total-and-balance check after every adjustment rather than assuming the previous lifter left the arrangement you expected.'),
section('Understand a rounded target',
'A percentage-based plan often produces a number between available increments. A coach might intentionally round that target to a practical load. The closest lower weight shown here is an equipment result, not a judgement that the original programme was badly designed. Keep the intended effort in mind when deciding whether a small difference is meaningful.',
'For a lift that progresses slowly, a small increment may be useful. Microplates can make a target loadable without a large jump. Before buying them, check that the hole size fits your bar and that a matched pair is available. Enter the actual denominations the tool supports; do not select a larger plate and pretend it represents several smaller ones.',
'If your target is below the bar weight, no plate arrangement can make the bar lighter. Choose a lighter suitable bar or another exercise setup with your coach. The bare-bar output is not the requested target. That distinction matters for beginners and for technical practice where the desired load may be much smaller than a standard bar.'),
section('Keep kilograms and pounds distinct',
'A twenty-kilogram plate is close to, but not identical to, a forty-five-pound plate. A twenty-kilogram bar is also not exactly a forty-five-pound bar. The unit switch changes the assumed equipment set as well as converting the target. Expect the actual loadable target to move slightly because the available increments change.',
'Do not convert the plate labels individually and then round each one before adding them. That can create a different total from converting the complete loaded bar. Add the equipment in its native unit first, then convert the total when needed for a log. Record which system the equipment uses so future comparisons remain understandable.',
'Mixed-unit equipment requires a separate arithmetic check. This interface assumes one unit system for the selected bar and plate set. If your gym combines kilogram bumpers with pound iron plates, convert the real combination carefully rather than expecting a single checkbox selection to represent it. Keep equal loads on both sides regardless of the labels printed on the plates.'),
section('Make the final check part of the routine',
'Before each work set, confirm the exercise, bar, target, plate order and matching sleeves. Ask for help when changing a heavy or awkward setup. Use appropriate rack safeties and a spotter where the lift and setting call for them. Accurate arithmetic is useful, but it does not replace safe setup or the skill required for the exercise.',
'After the set, log the load actually used. If the nearest practical weight differed from the planned percentage, preserve both the plan and the completed result. That makes the next adjustment easier and prevents a rounded load from being mistaken for a different strength level. A good training record explains what happened, including small equipment constraints.')]
}
EXTRA['rpe-calculator'].append(section('Keep the estimate attached to its exercise',
'A squat estimate belongs to the squat variation you performed. It should not become a front-squat, leg-press or deadlift maximum through a change of label. Even closely related lifts can differ enough to make that shortcut misleading. Keep separate records for movements with different technique requirements, and use a new baseline when you make a lasting change to the exercise.'))
EXTRA['rir-calculator'].append(section('Use the completed set as the record',
'The planned reserve target and the reported reserve are separate fields in your thinking, even if your training log displays them together. Preserve the target so you know what was intended, and record what happened without editing it to match. That distinction makes a second attempt useful: you can adjust the next session from a real observation instead of repeating an assumption.'))
EXTRA['tempo-calculator'].append(section('Three timing checks before using the result',
'First, check that every field is in seconds. Entering a count of three while actually lowering for roughly one second creates a different set from the written prescription. If the client counts quickly, teach a steadier rhythm or simplify the instruction. The calculation assumes the entered durations, not the words used while counting.',
'Second, check repetitions and sets separately. Eight repetitions across three sets means twenty-four repetitions in total. The calculator multiplies each repetition duration by eight for the set result, then by three for the session result. It does not assume that a pause between sets is part of the four-phase tempo. Add recovery time separately when scheduling the appointment.',
'Third, distinguish a prescribed pause from an unplanned interruption. Reracking midway through a set, adjusting a grip, or waiting for assistance changes the session but should not be hidden inside a tempo number. Record the interruption as a note. If it happens repeatedly, review whether the chosen load, setup or instruction is practical for the client.',
'These checks make the output easier to use in a real plan. The result should explain the intended execution in terms the client can follow, with a coach available to review differences. It should not turn every repetition into a timing exam or encourage a person to continue when control has been lost.'))
EXTRA['warm-up-set-calculator'].append(section('Save the ramp with the session',
'Put the preparation beside the working exercise rather than in a separate message the client must search for at the rack. Label warm-up sets clearly so they are not mistaken for additional working sets. Include the actual unit and the bar assumption. If the client uses another gym, ask them to confirm equipment before following the same numbers.',
'After several sessions, review whether the ramp helped. A client who understands the sequence and reaches the working sets comfortably needs a usable routine, not constant new calculations. Recalculate when the working target changes enough to make the old steps unsuitable, or when equipment and experience justify a different approach.'))
EXTRA['plate-loading-calculator'].append(section('Worked checks using the supplied plate sets',
'For a 100 kg target on a 20 kg bar, the plates account for 80 kg in total. Each sleeve therefore needs 40 kg. With the default kilogram set, a 25 kg plate and a 15 kg plate on each side make that amount. Check it as 20 plus twice 40, which equals 100. The result refers to each sleeve, not to a single forty-kilogram addition across the whole bar.',
'For a 102.5 kg target using the same bar, each sleeve needs 41.25 kg. A 25 kg plate, a 15 kg plate and a 1.25 kg plate make 41.25. Twice that amount plus the bar produces 102.5. If the small plates are not available, the exact target cannot be made from those larger increments. The tool reports the lower load it can assemble rather than inventing a plate.',
'For a 225 lb target on a 45 lb bar, the sleeves together need 180 lb. That is 90 lb per side, or two 45 lb plates on each sleeve. Adding a single extra 2.5 lb plate to both sides increases the whole bar by 5 lb, not 2.5. This is why plate increments and total-load increments must be distinguished when planning a small progression.',
'For a 135 lb target with the same bar, one 45 lb plate on each sleeve is sufficient. The phrase one plate often refers to that familiar arrangement, but it is ambiguous when the gym uses different denominations. Write the full load and unit in a client plan instead of relying on shorthand. Someone reading the plan in a kilogram gym should not need to guess which plate the coach intended.',
'When checking a result, write the equation in the order you will inspect the equipment: bar plus left sleeve plus right sleeve. The two sleeve totals should match before you add them. A correct overall total could otherwise conceal an unequal arrangement. Balanced loading is a separate condition from reaching the desired total, and both need to be true before a set begins.',
'The tool does not know whether a plate is calibrated, whether a bar is damaged, or whether the selected arrangement fits inside the collars. Those are physical checks. If a combination does not fit, use another balanced arrangement or a different appropriate setup. Do not leave collars insecure to accommodate a calculated result. The number is a planning aid, and the actual equipment remains the final constraint.'))
EXTRA['tempo-calculator'].append(section('Recheck after changing the prescription', 'When you change the number of sets or repetitions, read the new total before saving the plan. A small timing change repeated across many sets can noticeably change how long the movement portion takes.'))
EXTRA['plate-loading-calculator'].append(section('Confirm again when equipment changes', 'Recalculate after choosing another bar rather than leaving the previous result in place. Two bars that look similar may have different masses. The same plate arrangement then produces a different total, even though the sleeves look unchanged. Check the manufacturer label or ask the gym staff when the bar weight is uncertain.'))
